 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**No, it is best for your mother to skip her outdoor walk.** Singapore is experiencing significant haze, and air quality levels are unsafe for seniors. \n\n*(Note: It is already past noon in Singapore, so the morning window has passed—and walking in the afternoon is also strongly discouraged given the air quality and midday heat.)*\n\n### Air Quality & Haze Status\n* **24-Hour PSI:** The National Environment Agency (NEA) forecasted the 24-hour Pollutant Standards Index (PSI) to be between **150 and 230 (Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy)**. Morning readings across the island ranged from **142 to 187**.\n* **1-Hour PM2.5:** Hourly PM2.5 concentrations are in the **Elevated** band across most of Singapore, and climbed into the **High** band (158 µg/m³) in western Singapore.\n\n### Official NEA Health Advisory for Seniors\nAt 70 years old, your mother falls into the NEA's vulnerable group (elderly):\n* **General Planning:** With PSI in the *Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy* range, the NEA advises elderly individuals to **minimise all outdoor activity**.\n* **Immediate Activity (PM2.5):** When 1-hour PM2.5 readings are *Elevated*, seniors should avoid strenuous outdoor exercise. Where PM2.5 reaches the *High* band, elderly individuals should **avoid all outdoor activity**.\n* **Pre-existing Conditions:** If your mother has any chronic heart or lung conditions, the NEA advises avoiding outdoor activity completely.\n\n### What She Should Do Instead\n* **Stay Indoors:** She should do light indoor exercises or stretching at home with the windows closed and an air purifier running (if available).\n* **If She Must Go Out:** If she has to step outside for essential errands, she should keep the trip brief and wear an **N95 mask** to reduce exposure to fine particulate matter.\n* **Planning for Tomorrow Morning:** Showers and shifting winds may bring slight improvements, though haze from Sumatra and Kalimantan may persist. Before she heads out for a walk tomorrow morning, check the live **1-hour PM2.5 readings** for her specific region on the [NEA Haze Microsite](https://www.haze.gov.sg) or the **myENV** mobile app.",
